<releaseinfo></releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>
&i18n-kapplication; is a chat client for AI language models.
</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>KDE</keyword>
<keyword>AI</keyword>
<keyword>chat</keyword>
<keyword>Ollama</keyword>
<keyword>LLM</keyword>
<keyword>MCP</keyword>
<keyword>privacy</keyword>
</keywordset>
</bookinfo>

<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>&i18n-kapplication; is a chat application that lets you talk with AI language
models, either running locally with Ollama or provided by an online AI service.</para>

<para>Conversations are stored in a local database on your computer, so they are
still there the next time you start the application. You can browse them, search
through them, organize them in projects and with tags, archive the ones you do not
need any more, and export them to a file. When you do not want a conversation to be
kept at all, you can use an ephemeral discussion instead.</para>

<para>&i18n-kapplication; does not provide a model of its own: it talks to a model
provider that you configure. Nothing is sent anywhere until you have set up such a
provider and asked a question.</para>

</chapter>

<chapter id="getting-started">
<title>Getting Started</title>

<para>Before you can ask anything, &i18n-kapplication; needs at least one
<emphasis>instance</emphasis>. An instance is a configured connection to a model
provider: a local server running on your own machine, or an online service.</para>

<para>The first time you start the application without any instance, it offers to
create one. You can also open
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure
&i18n-kapplication;…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> and go to the
<guilabel>Instances</guilabel> page.</para>

<sect1 id="local-model">
<title>Using a local model</title>

<para>Running the model on your own computer keeps your questions on your machine.
&i18n-kapplication; can use local servers such as
<application>Ollama</application>, <application>llama.cpp</application> or
<application>LM Studio</application>.</para>

<para>Install and start the server first, then add an instance of the matching type.
The default address of a local <application>Ollama</application> server is already
filled in. If <application>Ollama</application> is installed but not running,
&i18n-kapplication; offers to start it for you; you can also do this from the command
line with <userinput><command>kaichat</command>
<option>--start-ollama</option></userinput>.</para>

<para>Once the instance is connected you still need a model. Models can be
downloaded from within the instance configuration, and &i18n-kapplication; notifies
you when a download is finished.</para>

</sect1>

<sect1 id="online-service">
<title>Using an online service</title>

<para>&i18n-kapplication; can also talk to hosted services, among them
Ollama Cloud, OpenAI, Anthropic, Mistral AI, Groq, Grok, Qwen, OpenRouter,
DeepInfra, Novita AI, Cerebras and other providers with an OpenAI-compatible
interface. The exact list depends on the plugins installed on your system; run
<userinput><command>kaichat</command> <option>--plugin-info</option></userinput> to
see it.</para>

<para>Create an instance of the matching type and paste the API key given to you by
the service. Be aware that with an online service your questions, and any file you
attach, are sent to that provider and are subject to its own privacy policy.</para>

</sect1>

</chapter>

<chapter id="using-kapp">
<title>Using &i18n-kapplication;</title>

<sect1 id="main-window">
<title>The main window</title>

<para>The window is split in three parts:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>the list of your chats, on the left. It can be collapsed with the
button on the splitter;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>the conversation itself, on the right, where your questions and the
answers of the model are shown;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>the input field at the bottom, where you type your
question.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>Above them, a header bar lets you start a new chat or an ephemeral discussion,
mark the current chat as a favorite and search in the current conversation. The status bar at the bottom shows what the model is currently doing,
for instance which tool it is calling.</para>

</sect1>

<sect1 id="chatting">
<title>Asking questions</title>

<para>Type your question in the input field and validate it. The answer appears in
the conversation view as it is being generated, and a notification is shown when a
long answer is finished.</para>

<para>You can attach files to a question, and enable the tools the model is allowed
to call while answering it, including the ones provided by your MCP servers (see
<xref linkend="mcp-servers"/>). Each chat keeps its own history, which is sent along
with your new question so that the model knows the context.</para>

</sect1>

<sect1 id="managing-chats">
<title>Managing chats</title>

<para>Chats are created from the header bar, or with
<userinput><command>kaichat</command> <option>--new-chat</option>
<replaceable>title</replaceable></userinput>.</para>

<para>Right-click a chat in the list to act on it:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Modify…</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Change the title of the chat.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Duplicate…</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Create a copy of the chat, for instance to try another direction
without losing the original conversation.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Set as Favorite</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Show the chat in the <guilabel>Favorite</guilabel> section at the top
of the list. The same can be done with the <guibutton>Favorite</guibutton> button of
the header bar.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guisubmenu>Project</guisubmenu></term>
<listitem><para>Move the chat into a project, or out of it with
<guimenuitem>No Project</guimenuitem>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guisubmenu>Tags</guisubmenu></term>
<listitem><para>Add tags to the chat or remove them.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Archive</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Hide the chat from the list without deleting it.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Remove…</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Delete the chat and its messages for good.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>The other chats are grouped by age: today, the last seven days, the last thirty
days and older.</para>

<para>A conversation can also be forked from one of its messages with the
<guibutton>Fork</guibutton> button shown next to it. This starts a new chat
containing the conversation up to that message, and leaves the original chat
untouched.</para>

<para>A chat you do not need any more can be archived instead of being deleted.
Archived chats are hidden from the list; show them again with
<menuchoice><guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Show
Archive</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.</para>

<para>To look for something you asked or read some time ago, use
<menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>F</keycap></keycombo></shortcut>
<guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Search in Database…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.
This searches the whole stored history, not only the chat you are looking at. The
number of matches is shown in the status bar.</para>

</sect1>

<sect1 id="projects-tags">
<title>Projects and tags</title>

<para>Projects and tags are two ways of organizing your chats. A chat belongs to at
most one project, which is useful to keep together the conversations about the same
piece of work. A chat can have any number of tags.</para>

<para>Both are created, renamed and removed with <guimenuitem>Manage
Projects…</guimenuitem> and <guimenuitem>Manage Tags…</guimenuitem>, in the
<guisubmenu>Project</guisubmenu> and <guisubmenu>Tags</guisubmenu> submenus of the
context menu of the chat list. Removing a project does not remove its chats: they are
kept, outside of any project.</para>

<para>The tag selector above the chat list only shows the chats which have one of the
selected tags. Select no tag to see all of them again.</para>

</sect1>

<sect1 id="ephemeral-chats">
<title>Ephemeral discussions</title>

<para>An ephemeral discussion is a chat which is never written to the database. Use
it for a question you do not want to keep: once you close &i18n-kapplication;, it is
gone. Start one with the <guibutton>Ephemeral Discussion</guibutton> button of the
header bar, or with <userinput><command>kaichat</command>
<option>--ephemeral-chat</option> <replaceable>title</replaceable></userinput>.
Ephemeral discussions are shown in their own section at the top of the chat
list.</para>

<para>If you change your mind, the <guibutton>Save Discussion in
Database</guibutton> button of the header bar turns it into a normal chat.</para>

<para>Keep in mind that this only concerns what is stored on your computer: with an
online service, your questions are still sent to that provider.</para>

</sect1>

<sect1 id="quick-ask">
<title>Quick Ask</title>

<para>Quick Ask is a small dialog for a single question, without opening the main
window. Open it with
<menuchoice><guimenu>Tools</guimenu><guimenuitem>Open Quick
Ask…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>, with the
<keycombo action="simul">&Meta;<keycap>Space</keycap></keycombo> global shortcut, or
with <userinput><command>kaichat</command> <option>--quick-ask</option></userinput>.
Questions asked this way are not added to your chats, unless you keep the discussion
with the <guibutton>Save Discussion in Database</guibutton> button.</para>

</sect1>

<sect1 id="import-export">
<title>Importing and exporting chats</title>

<para>The current chat can be saved to a file with
<menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Export…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>,
in one of four formats:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Export as JSON…</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>A <filename class="extension">.kaichat</filename> file. This is the
only format that &i18n-kapplication; can read back.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Export as Markdown…</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>A text file keeping the formatting of the answers.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Export as Text…</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Plain text.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Export as Pdf…</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>A printable document.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>A previously exported <filename class="extension">.kaichat</filename> file is
read back with
<menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Import…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.
You can also open such a file from your file manager, or pass it on the command
line.</para>

</sect1>

<sect1 id="appearance">
<title>Adjusting the display</title>

<para>The text of the whole application can be made bigger or smaller with
<menuchoice><guimenu>View</guimenu><guisubmenu>Font Size</guisubmenu></menuchoice>,
or with <keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>+</keycap></keycombo> and
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>-</keycap></keycombo>. A fixed font can be
chosen in the configuration dialog instead.</para>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guisubmenu>Color
Scheme</guisubmenu></menuchoice> switches the colors of the window without changing
your desktop settings, and <keycap>F11</keycap> switches to full screen.</para>

<para>The menu bar can be hidden with
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Show
Menubar</guimenuitem></menuchoice>; the actions stay available through the hamburger
button of the toolbar.</para>

</sect1>

</chapter>

<chapter id="configuring">
<title>Configuring &i18n-kapplication;</title>

<para>The configuration dialog is opened with
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure
&i18n-kapplication;…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. Depending on how the application was
built, it has the following pages.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>General</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Whether the system tray icon is shown, and the system prompt sent to
the model at the beginning of every conversation.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Font</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Use a font of your choice instead of the system one.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Instances</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Add, remove and configure the connections to your model providers,
and manage the models available on them.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>MCP Servers</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Model Context Protocol servers, which give the model access to
external data and actions. See <xref linkend="mcp-servers"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Spell Checking</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Spell checking of what you type.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Accessibility</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Text-to-speech settings, when the application was built with
text-to-speech support.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Plugins</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Enable or disable the installed plugins and configure the tools the
model may use.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>User Feedback</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>How much information, if any, you want to send to the developers.
Nothing is sent unless you ask for it here.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Retention</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>How long your chats are kept. <guilabel>Retention Days</guilabel> is
the number of days after which old chats are removed; with the default value of 0,
they are kept for ever. <guilabel>Exclude Favorite Chats</guilabel>, enabled by
default, keeps your favorite chats whatever their age.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Privacy</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Shows where your chats are stored on disk. The path can be copied to
the clipboard with the button next to it, for instance to back up or delete the
database.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>Notifications are configured with
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure
Notifications…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.</para>

<para>Instances and MCP servers can also be configured outside of the application,
from the <guilabel>AI</guilabel> module of &systemsettings;.</para>

<sect1 id="mcp-servers">
<title>MCP servers</title>

<para>The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open standard which lets a model use
tools provided by external programs, called MCP servers: reading your files, querying
a database, searching a documentation, and so on. &i18n-kapplication; acts as an MCP
client: it connects to the servers you configure and offers their tools to the
model.</para>

<sect2 id="mcp-adding-server">
<title>Adding a server</title>

<para>Servers are managed on the <guilabel>MCP Servers</guilabel> page of the
configuration dialog. Use the <guibutton>Add Server…</guibutton> button, or the
context menu of the list, to add one; double-click a server or use
<guimenuitem>Edit…</guimenuitem> to modify it, and <guimenuitem>Remove
Server</guimenuitem> to delete it. The checkbox in front of each server enables or
disables it without losing its configuration.</para>

<para>Each server has a <guilabel>Name</guilabel>, an optional
<guilabel>Icon</guilabel> and a <guilabel>Type</guilabel>, which tells how
&i18n-kapplication; talks to it:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Standard IO</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The server is a program started by &i18n-kapplication; on your
computer. Enter the <guilabel>Command</guilabel> to run and its
<guilabel>Arguments</guilabel>, as given in the documentation of the
server.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Streamable HTTP</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The server is reached over the network at the given
<guilabel>URL</guilabel>. Additional <guilabel>Headers</guilabel>, for instance an
<literal>Authorization</literal> header holding an access token, can be added in
<quote>Name: Value</quote> form.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>SSE</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The older HTTP transport based on Server-Sent Events, still used by
some servers. Enter its <guilabel>URL</guilabel>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para><guilabel>Allowed folders</guilabel> lists the folders the server may work in.
The list is passed to the server, which is expected to restrict itself to these
folders; a server which accesses files usually needs at least one.</para>

<para>Changes are applied when you close the configuration dialog with
<guibutton>OK</guibutton> or <guibutton>Apply</guibutton>. MCP servers can also be
configured from the <guilabel>AI</guilabel> module of &systemsettings;; the list is
shared with the other applications using it.</para>

</sect2>

<sect2 id="mcp-using-tools">
<title>Using MCP tools in a chat</title>

<para>Every enabled server is shown as a button among the tools available under the
input field. Check the button to let the model use the tools of this server for your
next questions. The arrow next to it opens a menu listing these tools, from which you
can select only some of them; &i18n-kapplication; connects to the server the first
time this menu is opened, in order to know its tools.</para>

<para>While the model is calling a tool, its name is shown in the status bar, and the
result is sent back to the model to complete its answer.</para>

</sect2>

<sect2 id="mcp-confirmation">
<title>Confirmations and requests</title>

<para>A tool can do more than read data, so &i18n-kapplication; asks before running
one. The <guilabel>Run Tool</guilabel> dialog shows the server, the tool and the
arguments the model wants to pass to it:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Allow</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Run the tool this time.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Always Allow This Server</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Run the tool, and no longer ask for any tool of this
server.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Deny</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>Do not run the tool.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>The servers whose tools run without confirmation are checked in the
<guilabel>Run tools without confirmation</guilabel> list of the <guilabel>MCP
Servers</guilabel> page, where you can uncheck them again.</para>

<para>A server can also ask something of you or of the model while it works:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>it can ask you to fill in some information, in a <guilabel>Request
from</guilabel> dialog showing the fields it needs. <guibutton>Accept</guibutton>
sends your answer, <guibutton>Decline</guibutton> refuses to give
it;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>it can ask the model to generate some text for it. The
<guilabel>Sampling Request from MCP Server</guilabel> dialog shows what would be sent
to the model; nothing is sent unless you choose <guibutton>Send to
LLM</guibutton>.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>Only add servers you trust. A <guilabel>Standard IO</guilabel> server is a
program running with your own rights, and what a tool returns is read by the model
and, with an online service, sent to its provider along with your
conversation.</para>

</sect2>

</sect1>

<sect1 id="files">
<title>Files used</title>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><filename>~/.config/kaichatrc</filename></term>
<listitem><para>The settings of the application.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><filename class="directory">~/.local/share/kaichat/ai-database/</filename></term>
<listitem><para>The databases holding your chats and their messages. The exact
location is shown on the <guilabel>Privacy</guilabel> page of the configuration
dialog.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>The configuration of your instances contains the API keys of the online
services you use. Treat these files as you would treat a password.</para>

</sect1>

</chapter>

<chapter id="command-line">
<title>Command Line Options</title>

<para>&i18n-kapplication; runs as a single instance: starting it again while it is
already running passes the options to the window that is already open instead of
opening a second one.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--list-instances</option></term>
<listitem><para>Print the configured instances and exit.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--list-chats</option></term>
<listitem><para>Print the titles of the stored chats and exit.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--plugin-info</option></term>
<listitem><para>Print the available text and tool plugins and exit.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--new-chat</option> <replaceable>title</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Create a new chat with this title.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--ephemeral-chat</option> <replaceable>title</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Create a new ephemeral discussion with this title. It is not stored
in the database.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--switch-to-chat</option> <replaceable>name</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Switch to an existing chat, by title.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--switch-to-chat-from-id</option> <replaceable>id</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Switch to an existing chat, by identifier.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--ask-message</option> <replaceable>message</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Ask this question straight away.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--attach</option> <replaceable>url</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Attach a file to the question. Can be repeated.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--tool</option> <replaceable>name</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Allow the model to use this tool. Can be repeated.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--quick-ask</option></term>
<listitem><para>Open the Quick Ask dialog.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--import</option> <replaceable>file</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Import a chat from a JSON file.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--start-ollama</option></term>
<listitem><para>Start the local <application>Ollama</application> program.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--debug</option></term>
<listitem><para>Enable debug mode, which adds a <guimenu>Debug</guimenu> menu
showing the content of the database.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--feedback</option></term>
<listitem><para>List the information that can be sent as user feedback.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><replaceable>file…</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Chat files to import.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>On systems with &DBus;, a running &i18n-kapplication; also exposes the
<interfacename>org.kde.kaichat.Application</interfacename> interface, which offers
the same operations as the options above: listing chats, creating one, switching to
one, showing archived chats and asking a question.</para>

</chapter>

